    "ml-hardware": {
      "t": "Hardware for ML",
      "f": "Training vs inference",
      "g": "A4.1",
      "ib": "The computing resources needed for machine learning depend on the task: training large models needs high-performance parallel processors (GPUs, TPUs), large amounts of memory and storage, and often cloud data centres; inference needs far less and can run on edge devices using low-power processors such as NPUs.",
      "q": "Compare and contrast the hardware requirements for training a large language model and for running face recognition on a smartphone. [4]",
      "a": [
        "Both need processors that can carry out many calculations in parallel;",
        "training a large language model needs thousands of GPUs or TPUs, very large amounts of RAM and storage, usually in a data centre or the cloud;",
        "whereas face recognition on a phone is inference only, carried out by a small, low-power NPU on the device;",
        "the phone prioritises low latency, battery life and privacy (the face data stays on the device), whereas training prioritises raw processing power over cost and energy use."
      ],
      "h": 0,
      "p": "A4-machine-learning",
      "s": "hardware",
      "fig": "mlhw",
      "k": "hardware for machine learning"
    },
