      "f": "",
      "g": "CS",
      "ib": "Ways to make a trained model cheaper to run: pruning removes weights or neurons that contribute little; quantization stores weights with fewer bits (e.g. 32-bit floats to 8-bit integers); knowledge distillation trains a smaller or fewer-step “student” model to imitate a large “teacher”.",
      "q": "Outline two techniques that reduce the computational cost of generating images with a diffusion model. [4]",
      "a": [
        "Quantization: storing the model’s weights with fewer bits (e.g. 16 or 8 instead of 32)…",
        "…reduces memory and speeds up each step, with a small possible loss in quality.",
        "Distillation: training a student model to reproduce the output of the full model in far fewer sampling steps…",
        "…so each image needs, for example, 4 network evaluations instead of 50."
      ],
      "h": 0,
      "p": "Case-study",
      "s": "diffusion",
