    "ip-copyright": {
      "t": "Intellectual property and copyright",
      "f": "",
      "g": "CS",
      "ib": "Intellectual property is legally protected creative work, such as images, illustrations and photographs; copyright gives the creator control over copying and use. Training on images scraped without permission, and generating work close to an artist’s style or a specific image, raises infringement and fairness questions.",
      "q": "Discuss the intellectual property issues of training an image model on images collected from the internet. [6]",
      "a": [
        "Most images online are protected by copyright, and their creators have not given permission for them to be copied into a training set.",
        "Artists argue that models trained on their work can reproduce their style and compete with them without payment or credit.",
        "Developers argue that training learns general patterns rather than storing copies, and in some places this may count as a permitted use, but the law is unsettled and lawsuits are ongoing.",
        "Outputs can occasionally closely reproduce a training image or a trademarked character, exposing the user to legal risk.",
        "Mitigations include licensed or owned datasets, honouring opt-outs, filtering outputs and recording provenance.",
        "A balanced conclusion: a studio relying on client trust should use licensed data, even at higher cost."
      ],
      "h": 0,
      "p": "Case-study",
      "s": "ethics",
      "fig": "",
      "k": "licensed|licensing"
    },
